---
<Base title="Graphs · ITC 120" description="Graphs: vertices and edges, directed and weighted graphs, and how to store them in an adjacency matrix or adjacency list." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Midterm › Graphs</p>
    <p class="kicker">Graphs · Lesson 1 of 3</p>
    <h1>Graphs, and How to Store Them</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">Trees let each node have one parent. Graphs remove that rule: anything can connect to anything.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>say what a graph is and give two ways it differs from a tree;</li>
        <li>point out a vertex's neighbors, degree (in- and out-degree for arrows), a path, a cycle, and a loop in a drawing;</li>
        <li>tell from a drawing or a matrix whether a graph is <b>directed</b> and whether it is <b>weighted</b>;</li>
        <li>write the <b>adjacency matrix</b> and <b>adjacency list</b> of a small graph, and draw a graph from either one;</li>
        <li>choose the matrix or the list for a given graph and explain why, and read Java code that stores a graph both ways.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the linked lists lesson (for adjacency lists), the trees lessons, and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <!-- 1 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="what">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The idea</p>
      <h2>What a graph is</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Graph</p>
        <p>A <b>graph</b> is a non-linear data structure made of a set of <b>vertices</b> (also called nodes) and a set of <b>edges</b>. Each edge connects two vertices.</p>
      </div>
      <GraphFig g={small} label="A small graph: A connects to B and D; B connects to C and D" caption="Vertices: A, B, C, D   ·   Edges: A–B, A–D, B–C, B–D" />
      <details class="reveal"><summary>Extra: how books write a graph</summary><div class="flow">
        <p class="xtra-note"><b>Extra topic.</b> You may see this short math form in books. Open this if you are curious.</p>
        <p>Books often write a graph as <b>G = (V, E)</b>: V is the set of vertices, E is the set of edges. Here, a <b>set</b> just means a group of items with no repeats, listed inside curly braces &#123; &#125;. (Sets get their own lesson later.) The graph above is V = &#123;A, B, C, D&#125; and E = &#123;A–B, A–D, B–C, B–D&#125;.</p>
      </div></details>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Graphs vs. trees</h3>
        <p>You already know one kind of graph: a <b>tree</b> is a graph with special rules (one root, every other node has exactly one parent, no cycles). A general graph drops those rules:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Tree</th><th>Graph</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Root?</b></td><td>yes, one</td><td>no root</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Parents?</b></td><td>every node except the root has exactly one</td><td>no parent/child idea; a vertex can connect to any number of others</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Cycles?</b></td><td>never</td><td>allowed (you can go around and come back)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Connected?</b></td><td>always</td><td>not always; some parts can be separate</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>So: <b>every tree is a graph, but not every graph is a tree.</b></p>
      </div>

      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Where you see graphs in IT</p>
        <p>Graphs are everywhere in IT. Here are some examples:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Computer networks:</b> devices (vertices) connected by cables or Wi-Fi links (edges).</li>
          <li><b>Social media:</b> people (vertices) and friendships (edges). "People you may know" are often friends of your friends.</li>
          <li><b>Maps and delivery apps:</b> places (vertices) and roads (edges), used to find a route.</li>
          <li><b>The web:</b> pages (vertices) and links (edges) from one page to another.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={graphsInIT()} /><figcaption>The four examples as small graphs. On social media, you are not friends with Bea yet (dashed line), but two of your friends are, so the app may suggest her. On the map, the numbers are travel minutes (made-up), so it is a <b>weighted</b> graph. On the web, a link goes one way, so the edges are arrows (a <b>directed</b> graph). You will meet both words in Part 2.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="words">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Vocabulary</p>
      <h2>Graph terminology</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>These are the basic terms used with graphs. The examples refer to the small graph above. <b>Tap any row</b> to see it in the graph (on a computer, just point at the row).</p>
      <div class="terms-ref">
        <aside class="tr-side" aria-label="The small graph, for reference">
          <GraphFig g={small} label="The small graph: A connects to B and D; B connects to C and D" />
          <p class="tr-note small">Orange = the term. Gold = the related vertices.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="tr-main scroll-x">
          <table class="tbl tr-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A" data-hl2="" data-e=""><td><b>Vertex</b> (node)</td><td>one item in the graph</td><td>A</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,B" data-hl2="" data-e="A-B"><td><b>Edge</b></td><td>a connection between two vertices</td><td>A–B</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="B,C" data-hl2="" data-e="B-C"><td><b>Adjacent</b> (neighbors)</td><td>two vertices joined by an edge</td><td>B and C are adjacent; A and C are not</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="B" data-hl2="A,C,D" data-e="A-B,B-C,B-D"><td><b>Degree</b></td><td>the number of edges touching a vertex</td><td>B has degree 3</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,C" data-hl2="B" data-e="A-B,B-C"><td><b>Path</b></td><td>a sequence of vertices, each joined to the next by an edge</td><td>A → B → C</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A" data-hl2="B,D" data-e="A-B,B-D,A-D"><td><b>Cycle</b></td><td>a path that starts and ends at the same vertex, without repeating any edge or any other vertex</td><td>A → B → D → A</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="" data-hl2="" data-e=""><td><b>Loop</b></td><td>an edge from a vertex to itself</td><td>(none here)</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,B,C,D" data-hl2="" data-e="A-B,A-D,B-C,B-D"><td><b>Connected</b></td><td>every vertex can be reached from every other vertex</td><td>this graph is connected</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Directed or undirected?</h3>
        <p>Graphs can be described in two ways, depending on whether edges have a direction:</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <figure class="fig">
            <p class="fig-cap">Undirected</p>
            <GraphFig g={small} label="Undirected graph" />
            <p class="small">Edges have <b>no direction</b>. A–B means you can go A to B <i>and</i> B to A. Like Facebook friends: if you are my friend, I am yours.</p>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <p class="fig-cap">Directed</p>
            <GraphFig g={smallDir} label="Directed graph with arrows" />
            <p class="small">Each edge is an <b>arrow</b>. A → B lets you go from A to B, but not back. Like following someone on TikTok: they do not have to follow you back.</p>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>In a directed graph, degree splits in two. The <b>out-degree</b> counts arrows leaving a vertex; the <b>in-degree</b> counts arrows coming in. In the directed graph above, A has out-degree 2 (to B and D) and in-degree 1 (from C).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Weighted or unweighted?</h3>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="two-col">
            <GraphFig g={weighted} label="Weighted graph with numbers on the edges" />
            <div class="flow">
              <p>In a <b>weighted</b> graph, each edge carries a number called its <b>weight</b>. It can mean distance, cost, or time.</p>
              <p>Here the weights are travel times in minutes (made-up numbers). From A to C, the path A → D → B → C takes 4 + 5 + 12 = 21 minutes, but A → B → C takes 7 + 12 = 19.</p>
              <p>An <b>unweighted</b> graph has no numbers: every edge counts the same.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Loops</h3>
        <GraphFig g={withLoop} label="Directed graph where C has an arrow to itself" />
        <p>C has a <b>loop</b>: an edge that leaves C and comes right back to C. Think of a web page that links to itself (like a "back to top" link).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="matrix">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Storing a graph, way 1</p>
      <h2>Adjacency matrix</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>The circles-and-lines pictures in these lessons are drawn for <b>people</b>: our eyes can follow the lines and see who connects to whom. A computer cannot look at a picture. It needs the same facts written down as <b>data</b> in memory: which vertex has an edge to which.</p>
      <p>Where each circle sits on the page does not matter. Move the circles around and it is still the same graph, because only the connections count. So the computer stores only the connections. There are two common ways to do it: the <b>adjacency matrix</b> (this part) and the <b>adjacency list</b> (Part 4). Both hold exactly the same graph as the drawing. The matrix is stored in a <b>2D array</b>, so first, a quick look at one.</p>
      <div class="sub" id="two-d">
        <h3>First: a 2D array</h3>
        <p>In the <a href={base + '/itc120/arrays/'}>Arrays</a> lesson, an array was <b>one row</b> of slots. Some data needs a <b>grid</b>: rows <i>and</i> columns. Think of the seat plan in your classroom. To point to one seat, you say two numbers: its row and its column.</p>
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">2D array</p><p>A <b>2D (two-dimensional) array</b> is a grid of slots, all holding the same type. Each slot has <b>two</b> indexes: <b>[row][column]</b>. Both count from 0, like a normal array.</p></div>
        <table class="grid2d" aria-label="A 2D array named seats with 3 rows and 4 columns. Every slot holds 0 except seats[1][2], row 1, column 2, which holds 7.">
          <thead><tr><th class="g2-corner">row ↓ / col →</th>{[0, 1, 2, 3].map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>{[0, 1, 2].map((r) => <tr><th>{r}</th>{[0, 1, 2, 3].map((c) => <td class={r === 1 && c === 2 ? 'hot' : ''}>{r === 1 && c === 2 ? 7 : 0}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="fig-cap"><code>seats[1][2]</code> is row 1, column 2 (the orange slot). Every other slot still holds 0.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(twoD)} /></pre>
        <p>Here is how to read each line aloud:</p>
        <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl ra">
          <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Read it as</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>int[][] seats = new int[3][4];</code></td><td>"make a grid named seats with 3 rows and 4 columns." Every slot starts at 0. The two pairs of <code>[ ]</code> mean "rows, then columns."</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>seats[1][2] = 7;</code></td><td>"go to row 1, then to column 2, and store 7 there."</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>seats.length</code></td><td>"how many rows": 3.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>seats[0].length</code></td><td>"how many columns in row 0": 4.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <div class="trap"><p class="note-label">Row first, then column</p><p><code>seats[2][1]</code> is a different slot: row 2, column 1. It still holds 0.</p></div>
        <p>An adjacency matrix is exactly this kind of grid. Its rows are the vertices, its columns are the same vertices again, and each slot answers one question: is there an edge from this row's vertex to this column's vertex?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Adjacency matrix</p>
        <p>An <b>adjacency matrix</b> is a 2D array with one row and one column for each vertex. The cell <b>[row][column]</b> is <b>1</b> if there is an edge <b>from</b> the row vertex <b>to</b> the column vertex, and <b>0</b> if there is not.</p>
      </div>
      <GraphFig g={smallDir} show={['graph', 'matrix']} label="The directed graph and its adjacency matrix" />
      <p><b>How to read it:</b> pick a row and read across. Row A is <span class="mono">0 1 0 1</span>, so A has edges to B and D. To find edges coming <i>into</i> a vertex, read <b>down its column</b>: column A has a 1 in row C, so C → A.</p>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Three things to notice</h3>
        <p>The matrix shows you the type of graph. Look for these three things:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Undirected = mirror image.</b> Every undirected edge A–B is stored twice, as [A][B] and [B][A]. So the matrix matches itself across the diagonal (it is <b>symmetric</b>).</li>
          <li><b>The diagonal means loops.</b> Cell [C][C] is an edge from C to itself. If the diagonal is all 0, there are no loops.</li>
          <li><b>Weights replace the 1s.</b> In a weighted graph, the cell stores the weight instead of 1. In this course, 0 still means "no edge". Some books write ∞ or leave the cell blank instead, because a real edge could have weight 0.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="two-col">
          <figure class="fig">
            <p class="fig-cap">Undirected: symmetric</p>
            <GraphFig g={small} show={['matrix']} />
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <p class="fig-cap">Weighted: numbers</p>
            <GraphFig g={weighted} show={['matrix']} />
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="list">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Storing a graph, way 2</p>
      <h2>Adjacency list</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Adjacency list</p>
        <p>An <b>adjacency list</b> gives each vertex its own list of the vertices it has an edge to. The lists are often linked lists, so each one ends with <code>null</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <GraphFig g={smallDir} show={['graph', 'list']} label="The directed graph and its adjacency list" />
      <p>This should look familiar: each row is a <Peek label="linked list" title="From the Linked lists lesson" html={llPeek} /> from the linked lists lesson. A's list is <span class="mono">B → D → null</span>, so A has edges to B and D.</p>
      <p>For an <b>undirected</b> graph, each edge appears in <b>two</b> lists: A–B puts B in A's list and A in B's list. For a <b>weighted</b> graph, each cell also stores the weight, for example (B, 7).</p>

      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Exam traps</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A row (or a list) shows edges going <b>out</b>. To find edges coming <b>in</b>, read the column, or look for the vertex inside the <i>other</i> lists.</li>
          <li>The order inside a list does not change the graph. A → B → D and A → D → B describe the same edges.</li>
          <li>A vertex with an empty list (just <code>null</code>) has no outgoing edges, but it can still have incoming ones.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="build">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Play</p>
      <h2>Build your own</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>All three views below show the same graph. Change one and watch the others follow. Try these:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>In <b>undirected</b> mode, tap [B][E]. Two cells change. Why?</li>
        <li>Switch to <b>directed</b> and tap [E][D]. Now only one cell changes.</li>
        <li>Tap a diagonal cell to make a loop.</li>
      </ol>
      <MatrixLab id="mxlab" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="compare">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · Choosing</p>
      <h2>Matrix or list?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Both store the same graph. They differ in memory and in which questions they answer quickly:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Adjacency matrix</th><th>Adjacency list</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Memory used</b></td><td>always vertices × vertices cells (100 vertices → 10,000 cells), even with few edges</td><td>one list per vertex, plus one cell per edge that exists (two for an undirected edge)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>"Is there an edge A → B?"</b></td><td>look at one cell: [A][B]. Fast.</td><td>walk through A's list until you find B</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>"Who are A's neighbors?"</b></td><td>scan the whole row, including all the 0s</td><td>walk A's list: only real neighbors</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Best when</b></td><td>the graph is <b>dense</b> (many edges) or small</td><td>the graph is <b>sparse</b> (few edges per vertex)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="sticky">
        <p class="note-label">Think of your class</p>
        <p>To record who is friends with whom in a class of 40, a matrix is a 40 × 40 grid: 1,600 boxes, mostly empty. A list is like each student writing just their friends' names on an index card. Most real graphs (social networks, roads) are sparse, so lists are used more often.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="read">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 7 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Reading practice</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Exams often give you only the matrix or only the list, with no drawing. Use these two views of the same graph to answer the questions. Try sketching the graph on paper first.</p>
      <figure class="fig">
        <GraphFig g={practiceG} show={['matrix', 'list']} />
      </figure>
      <Quiz id="quiz-read" questions={readQuiz} />
      <details class="reveal">
        <summary>Show the drawing</summary>
        <div><GraphFig g={practiceG} label="The practice graph drawn out" /></div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="java">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 8 · In code</p>
      <h2>Graphs in Java</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>In Java, we usually number the vertices 0, 1, 2, … and keep their names in a separate array. So A is 0, B is 1, C is 2, and D is 3.</p>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Adjacency matrix: a 2D array</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(code1)} /></pre>
        <TryIt cls="MatrixGraph" />
        <p>Here is how to read the new lines aloud:</p>
        <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl ra">
          <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Read it as</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>static char[] names = &#123;'A', 'B', 'C', 'D'&#125;;</code></td><td>"names is an array of <b>char</b> values, holding A, B, C, D." A <code>char</code> is one character, written in single quotes: <code>'A'</code>. (Double quotes, <code>"A"</code>, make a String.) So <code>names[1]</code> is <code>'B'</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>static int n = names.length;</code></td><td>"n is the number of names": 4. Because it is <code>static</code>, every method in the class can use it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>static int[][] adj = new int[n][n];</code></td><td>"make a grid named adj with n rows and n columns." Every cell starts at 0 (no edges yet). This is the <a href="#two-d">2D array</a> from Part 3.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>adj[from][to] = 1;</code></td><td>"in row <i>from</i>, column <i>to</i>, store 1": there is now an edge from <i>from</i> to <i>to</i>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>if (adj[1][j] == 1)</code></td><td>"if row 1 (B) has a 1 in column j": then vertex j is a neighbor of B.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="java-list">
        <h3>Adjacency list: a list of lists</h3>
        <p><code>ArrayList</code> is Java's built-in resizable array: you can keep calling <code>add</code> and it grows. Here, we keep one <code>ArrayList</code> per vertex. Each inner list plays the role of the linked lists in our pictures.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(code2)} /></pre>
        <TryIt cls="ListGraph" />
        <p>Here is how to read the new lines aloud:</p>
        <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl ra">
          <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Read it as</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>import java.util.ArrayList;</code></td><td>"let this program use Java's ArrayList class." ArrayList is in Java's <code>java.util</code> package, so we must import it first.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ArrayList&lt;ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; list</code></td><td>"list is an ArrayList whose items are ArrayLists of Integers": a list of lists. The type inside <code>&lt; &gt;</code> says what a list holds.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>= new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();</code></td><td>"make a new, empty one." The empty <code>&lt;&gt;</code> copies the types from the left side.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>list.add(new ArrayList&lt;&gt;());</code></td><td>"add one new, empty inner list at the end." The loop does this n times: index 0 is A's list, index 1 is B's list, and so on.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>list.get(from).add(to);</code></td><td>Read it left to right: "get vertex <i>from</i>'s list, then add <i>to</i> at its end."</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>for (int j : list.get(1))</code></td><td>"for each number j in B's list." This is a <b>for-each loop</b>: no index and no <code>i++</code>. It visits every item in order and stops after the last one.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="small muted">Why <code>Integer</code> and not <code>int</code>? The types inside <code>&lt; &gt;</code> must be classes. <code>Integer</code> is the class version of <code>int</code>, and Java converts between the two for you, so <code>add(to)</code> works with a plain int, and <code>int j</code> in the for-each loop gets a plain int back.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Notice the difference when listing B's neighbors: the matrix loop checks all <code>n</code> cells in row 1, while the list loop only visits B's real neighbors.</p>
      <p>One more difference: the matrix loop always gives neighbors in index order (A, B, C, …), but an <code>ArrayList</code> keeps them in the order the edges were added. Here both give A C D. In the next lessons, this order matters: it decides which neighbor BFS and DFS visit first.</p>
      <div class="paper flow" id="task">
        <p class="note-label">Your coding task: turn a matrix into a list</p>
        <p>In <Peek label="Reading practice" title="From Part 7 · Reading practice" html={practicePeek} />, you read a matrix and wrote its adjacency list by hand: for each row, list the columns that hold a 1. Now write that in Java.</p>
        <p>Finish <code>toList</code> in about 7 lines where it says YOUR CODE. You need a loop inside a loop: the outer loop picks row <code>i</code>, and the inner loop checks each column <code>j</code> of that row. To add to a vertex's list, use <code>list.get(i).add(j)</code>, as in the <a href="#java-list">adjacency list program</a> above. Test 1 is the directed graph from Reading practice. Test 2 is the undirected graph from this part, with a vertex E that has no edges. Run it: the printed lines must match the expected lines in the comments.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskCode)} /></pre>
        <TryIt />
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show one possible answer</summary><div class="flow">
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskAns)} /></pre>
          <p>Output: the ten lines in the comments, from <code>A: C D</code> to the last <code>E:</code>. E's list is empty in both tests, because E's row is all 0s.</p>
          <p>Row <code>i</code> is the <b>from</b> vertex and column <code>j</code> is the <b>to</b> vertex, so a 1 in <code>adj[i][j]</code> goes into <b>i's</b> list. And because <code>j</code> counts up from 0, every list comes out in alphabetical order. BFS and DFS use that order in the next lessons.</p>
          <p>A common mistake is <code>list.get(j).add(i)</code>, the wrong way around. Test 2 still passes: an undirected matrix is a <a href="#matrix">mirror image</a>, so every edge is stored both ways. Test 1 catches it. It prints <code>A: B</code>, <code>B: C D</code>, <code>C: A</code>, <code>D: A</code>, <code>E: D</code>: the arrows coming <b>into</b> each vertex, not going out. That is why the tests include a directed graph.</p>
        </div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 9 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-graphs" questions={checkQuiz} />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A graph is a set of vertices and edges. Trees are a special kind of graph.</li>
          <li>Directed edges are arrows (one way). Weighted edges carry numbers.</li>
          <li>Adjacency matrix: [row][column] = 1 means an edge from row to column. Undirected → symmetric. Diagonal → loops.</li>
          <li>Adjacency list: each vertex has a list of where its edges go, ending in null.</li>
          <li>Matrix: quick edge checks, but lots of memory. List: saves memory for sparse graphs.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next lesson:</b> Breadth-first search (BFS), a way to visit every vertex of a graph, level by level.</p>
      <div class="refs small muted">
        <p class="note-label">References</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A. Bhargava, <i>Grokking Algorithms</i>. Manning, 2016, Chapter 6 (introduction to graphs).</li>
          <li>T. H. Cormen, C. E. Leiserson, R. L. Rivest, and C. Stein, <i>Introduction to Algorithms</i>, 4th ed. MIT Press, 2022, Section 20.1 (Representations of graphs).</li>
          <li>Programiz, "Graph Data Structure," "Adjacency Matrix," and "Adjacency List." <a href="https://www.programiz.com/dsa/graph">programiz.com/dsa/graph</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
